WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.000
we'll look at the posterior surface and

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:05.000
find a few important structures here. If

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:07.000
you think about blood coming into the

00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:09.000
heart, it has to come through the

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:12.000
superior and inferior vena cava. Once you've

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:14.000
turned the heart on its backside, now

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:16.000
you're actually looking at the right

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:18.000
ventricle here, and this would be the

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:20.000
right auricle. So I'm going to look for

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:22.000
the superior and inferior vena cava dumping

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:26.000
into this area, and sure enough, although

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:29.000
it has been cut on this one a little bit,

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:32.000
this is the superior and inferior

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:34.000
vena cava. If

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:37.000
you want to double check your work, you can

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:38.000
stick your finger in there and see how

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:41.000
my finger is now into the right auricle

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:43.000
and into the inside of the right

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:46.000
atrium. Again, this is cut right here. It's

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:48.000
been dissected. Normally this would be a

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:52.000
tube. So the superior vena cava enters right

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:55.000
here, and then the inferior vena cava enters

00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:57.000
right here. Never be afraid to stick your

00:00:57.000 --> 00:00:59.000
fingers in there to see where it goes,

00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:01.000
and then you'll know what vessel it is.

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:02.000
Okay, so that's how blood gets into the

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:05.000
right atrium, and then it goes down to

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:07.000
the right ventricle, and then it goes up

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:09.000
and out the pulmonary trunk to the lungs.

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:11.000
Then it comes back to the left side of

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:13.000
the heart. So since we're looking at the

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:14.000
back side of the heart, this would be the

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:17.000
left ventricle, which would make this the

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:20.000
left auricle up here. So if you want to

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:23.000
find a path going in to the left atrium,

00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:24.000
you would look right next to there and

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:27.000
see, and sure enough, this large opening

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:31.000
goes right into the left atrium. And what

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:34.000
this large opening is, is pulmonary

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:36.000
veins that have come together. So you had

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:39.000
two coming from over here and two coming

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:42.000
from this lung, and then those two, this

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:44.000
will be the left lung, the right lung,

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:47.000
and then those pulmonary veins dump into

00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:50.000
the left atrium.

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:50.070
Okay, so now we're ready to open
